Production Readiness Audit and Remediation Plan

Published Site URL: https://pythonprincessux.github.io/git414-production-readiness-deliverable/work/git414-515-module0/production-readiness-assessment-site/index.html

1. Audit Findings
Finding 1
ID and area: H1 HTML and content structure
Condition: visit.html | Line 28 | W3C Nu HTML Checker
Finding: The image does not have an alt attribute.
Evidence: W3C Nu HTML Checker returned: "One error: map image missing an alt attribute on line 28." The https://html.spec.whatwg.org/dev/images.html states: "The alt attribute must be specified, and its value must not be empty; the value must be an appropriate replacement for the image." Evidence: visit-html-validation.png.
Impact: People using screen readers cannot tell what the map shows and miss directions to the market.

Priority: High

Next step: Adding an alt attribute to describe what the map is showing.

Finding 2
ID and area: H2 HTML and content structure

Condition: vendors.html | Lines 45–66 | VS Code inspection

Finding: The vendor checklist table uses  <td> cells for its column labels and does not contain any <th> elements.

Evidence: The code shows column labels as <td> cells instead of <th> cells. Evidence: vendors-table-headers.png. The MDN Web Docs HTML Table Advanced Features and Accessibility guide states: "To make tables accessible, you need to define header cells using the <th> element."

Impact: The screen reader users hear the items in the table without column associations which makes the checklist harder to interpret.

Priority: Medium

Next step: Change the label cells to <th scope="col">.

Finding 3
ID and area: R1 CSS and responsive behavior

Condition: assets/styles.css | Lines 163–169 | VS Code inspection & Chrome DevTools, Responsive 375 × 812, preview scale 100%

Finding: The media line combines min-width: 980px and max-width: 760px with and, which keeps the responsive styles from applying.

Evidence: Inspection of assets/styles.css lines 163–169 shows @media (min-width: 980px) and (max-width: 760px). Evidence: impossible-media-query.png. In DevTools at 375 × 812, the homepage shows a scaled down desktop layout where the hero is in two columns and the feature cards are in three columns. Evidence: index-mobile-375.png.

Impact: Visitors using a mobile view see a shrunken desktop layout that is hard to navigate and hard to read.

Priority: High

Next step: Change the media line to a single condition that applies at narrow widths or mobile viewports, such as @media (max-width: 760px).

Finding 4
ID and area: R2 CSS and responsive behavior

Condition: signup.html | Chrome DevTools, Responsive view at 375 × 812, scale 100%

Finding: The form extends past the header and footer width, and the navigation overflows.

Evidence: Chrome DevTools shows .page { width: 1120px; }. Evidence: signup-mobile-375.png.

Impact: During testing, I could see that phone users have to scroll horizontally to see the entire form and complete the signup.

Priority: High

Next step: Replace the fixed width on .page with a responsive rule such as width: 100% and max-width: 1120px.

Finding 5
ID and area: R3 CSS and responsive behavior

Condition: index.html | Chrome on macOS, 200% browser zoom, DevTools closed

Finding: Paragraph text goes beyond the right edge of the viewport, requiring horizontal scrolling to read the full page.

Evidence: Browser inspection at 200% zoom showed text overflowing past the screen. Evidence: index-zoom-200.png.

Impact: Low-vision users who use zoom to view the page must scroll both horizontally and vertically back and forth to read all of the content.

Priority: High

Next step: Remove fixed container widths so text is readable within the zoom selected, here 200%.

Finding 6
ID and area: A1 Accessibility

Condition: index.html | Chrome on macOS, 100% zoom, Tab key navigation

Finding: Pressing Tab button showed link destinations in the browser's bottom status area, but no visible outline or highlight like expected.

Evidence: In assets/styles.css on lines 26–28, the stylesheet removes link focus outlines with a:focus { outline: 0; }. Evidence: keyboard-focus-css.png. The W3C WCAG 2.1 Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.7: Focus Visible states: "Any keyboard operable user interface has a mode of operation where the keyboard focus indicator is visible."

Impact: People navigating by keyboard keys cannot see which link is currently active.

Priority: High

Next step: Remove outline: 0; from a:focus and give users a visible focus indicator.

Finding 7
ID and area: A2 Accessibility

Condition: index.html, element p.eyebrow | Lighthouse in Chrome & DevTools color picker

Finding: Insufficient foreground/background contrast for p.eyebrow, the "Saturday Community Market" label.

Evidence: Lighthouse flagged an insufficient contrast. Evidence: index-lighthouse-contrast.png. Manual measurement in Chrome DevTools color picker gave me a contrast ratio of 4.04:1 for foreground color #c65f3d. Evidence: index-contrast-measurement.png.

Impact: People with low vision will have trouble reading the label.

Priority: Medium

Next step: Change the #c65f3d color to a darker shade that reaches at least 4.5:1 contrast against the background.

Finding 8
ID and area: D1 Discoverability and metadata

Condition: index.html, lines 3–7 | VS Code inspection

Finding: The homepage head contains a charset declaration, title, and stylesheet link, but no viewport meta tag or description at all.

Evidence: When I directly inspected the code in lines 3–7 in index.html I could see the missing tags. Evidence: index-head-metadata.png. The MDN Web Docs Viewport Meta Tag guide notes that omitting the viewport tag causes mobile browsers to render at a desktop width.

Impact: Mobile browsers treat the layout like a wide desktop screen. No custom page summary to show in the results.

Priority: High

Next step: Add <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"> and a descriptive <meta name="description"> to the head.

Finding 9
ID and area: D2 Discoverability and metadata

Condition: index.html | W3C Nu HTML Checker

Finding: Missing document language and missing section heading.

Evidence: W3C Nu HTML Checker returned: "Two warnings: missing document language and missing section heading."

Impact: Screen readers cannot see the proper language pronunciation rules, and users navigating by headings would miss sections that are not labeled at all.

Priority: Medium

Next step: Add lang="en" to the <html> element and add a heading to the section that does not have a label.

Finding 10
ID and area: P1 Performance, compatibility, validation, links, or release quality
Condition: signup.html | Form submission test via Live Server in Chrome
Finding: When I submitted the form, I noticed both times the signup page would return with cleared fields and submitted values in the URL query string, with no success confirmation displayed.
Evidence: Submitting the form reloaded signup.html with empty inputs and names and values to the browser address bar. Source inspection confirmed no explicit action or method attribute and no submission script.
Impact: Users cannot tell if their registration was received, and submitted information.
Priority: Medium
Next step: Add a form action, set method="POST", and add an on-screen confirmation message for submissions to make it more clear for the user/applicant.
2. Remediation Plan
First (High Priority)
Fix the media query (Finding R1): Rewrite the impossible media query on lines 163–169 of assets/styles.css so it would work on mobile devices for .hero, .feature-row, and .vendor-grid take effect.
Restore focus outlines (Finding A1): Delete a:focus { outline: 0; } in assets/styles.css so keyboard focus indicators are visible.
Add viewport meta tag (Finding D1): Insert <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"> in the <head> of all HTML pages.
Fix signup container width (Finding R2): Change .page { width: 1120px; } to a flexible width so the form fits on mobile screens.
Fix 200% zoom text overflow (Finding R3): Fix containers so paragraph text does not overflowwithout horizontal scrolling.
Add map alt text (Finding H1): Add an alt attribute to the map image on line 28 of visit.html.
Next (Medium Priority)
Add document language and section heading (Finding D2): Add lang="en" to index.html and place a heading in the section that is not labeled at all.
Add table headers (Finding H2): Change the vendor checklist label cells in vendors.html to <th scope="col">.
Fix label color contrast (Finding A2): Fix the #c65f3d color on p.eyebrow to meet the 4.5:1 ratio.
Set up form submission (Finding P1): Set method="POST", add an action, and show a confirmation on signup.html.

Later (Low Priority)
Add meta descriptions (Finding D1): Write summary descriptions for search engine results across the pages to make it easier for the visitor/user.

3. Technical Defense: Finding A1 (Visible Focus Indicator Removed)
Condition
On index.html, pressing Tab in Chrome on macOS at 100% zoom showed link destinations in the browser's bottom status area, but no visible outline or highlight appeared on the focused link.

Evidence
Inspection of assets/styles.css lines 26–28 shows that link focus outlines are removed with:

CSS
a:focus {
  outline: 0;
}


Testing confirmed that pressing Enter opened the linked page, showing that keyboard activation was still working while the visible focus failed. Evidence: keyboard-focus-css.png.

User Impact
Keyboard only users rely on the visual focus ring to know where they are on the page. 
Authority
W3C WCAG 2.1 Understanding Success Criterion 2.4.7: Focus Visible: Requires that "Any keyboard operable user interface has a mode of operation where the keyboard focus indicator is visible."
MDN Web Docs :focus pseudo-class: States: "Never remove the focus outline without replacing it with an accessible alternative focus style."

Recommended Next Step
Remove outline: 0; from assets/styles.css and provide a visible outline style for keyboard user, like a gold focus indicator we would typically see when tabbing through the page.


